#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:5
#: 41d7ba2ce7bd4d74aed9b578801700db
msgid "Vibration Damping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:7
#: 875287ec5c644d278f3651dbc7433a9a
msgid "Autopilots have accelerometers that are sensitive to vibrations. These accelerometer values are combined with barometer and GPS data to estimate the vehicle's position. With excessive vibrations, the estimate can be thrown off and lead to very bad performance in modes that rely on accurate positioning (e.g. on Copter: AltHold, Loiter, RTL, Guided, Position and Auto flight modes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:14
#: 2f4832d577fd49eeb4b34eb9c89bbf8f
msgid "Please refer to the :ref:`Measuring Vibration <common-measuring-vibration>` page for details of how to measure your vehicle's vibration levels and confirm they are within the acceptable range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:17
#: f0e29bcad76f49f5968998c51f942458
msgid "The goal of vibration damping is to reduce high and medium frequency vibrations while still allowing low frequency actual board movement to take place in concert with the airframe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:21
#: bb9daafad22844d4aa5829eaf90c1525
msgid "Double sided foam tape or Velcro has traditionally been used to attach the autopilot to the frame. In many cases foam tape or Velcro does not provide adequate vibration isolation because the mass of the autopilot is so small."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:28
#: b899feb5cccf41da8f8a69874b1be074
msgid "The examples and images in this article refer to Copter, but the information is also largely applicable to Plane and Rover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:32
#: 0629b6db54ef469084d3972afd24e46c
msgid "3M, Du-Bro or HobbyKing Foam"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:34
#: 507d275ea0184d709f2cf3624280da72
msgid "One of the following three types of foam should be cut into small 1cm ~ 2cm squares and attached to each of the four corners of the autopilot as described on the :ref:`Mounting the Autopilot wiki page <common-mounting-the-flight-controller>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:36
#: aeaa66f0d0ee4cc592a1a2b9c107947f
msgid "3M Foam is sticky on both sides and comes pre-cut so that individual squares can be easily attached to the autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:37
#: c30eaabea737430eb233011cd4c0e682
msgid "`sold by mRobotics <https://store.mrobotics.io/product-p/mro-pxfoams-mr.htm>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:38
#: 73160c6924dc43048792fd1093a2cdd1
msgid "`sold by Holybro <https://holybro.com/products/foam-pads-20pcs>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:39
#: 88c04c69eae341538d7579c6563c1e5b
msgid "`Du-Bro 1/4\" R/C foam <https://www.dubro.com/products/r-c-protective-foam-rubber>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:40
#: 67589991ccb34cc6b438290c116f4377
msgid "`HobbyKing orange foam <https://hobbyking.com/en_us/anti-vibration-foam-orange-latex-190mm-x-140mm-x-6mm.html?___store=en_us>`__ (discontinued)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:42
#: 2d741b8ff42347c3a4f9a204719a411c
msgid "For the last two options \"carpet fixing tape\" will be required to attach the foam to the autopilot and vehicle frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:47
#: a55dff4530fa4c558684cbb63ea46191
msgid "For vehicles with :ref:`internal combustion engines <common-ice>`, the autopilot should be mounted on an intermediate plate with `self adhesive lead weights <https://www.amazon.com/Great-Planes-Segmented-Weights-6-Ounce/dp/B0015KLJE0>`__ added to increase its mass."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:50
#: 76c09fbac3124ce980b4875ad7f6bf49
msgid "Gel pads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:52
#: 8ec6223f820e4185bdf86e83eb1dcd7a
msgid "Cut one of the recommended gels into 1cm ~ 2cm squares and attach to each corner of the autopilot.  Possible gels include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:54
#: b7f68dc013f04ccfbeed27eaf7aaf0db
msgid "`Kyosho Zeal Gel Tape <https://www.amainhobbies.com/kyosho-zeal-vibration-absorption-gyro-reciever-mounting-gel-1-sheet-kyoz8006/p19713>`__ performs best and is available from Amazon, E-Bay and A-Main Hobbies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:55
#: 9c0ba0900508416d89a3418158be5b96
msgid "`United States Silicone Gel Tape and Pads (V10Z62MGT5 tape recommended) <http://www.vibrationmounts.com/RFQ/VM08010.htm>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:56
#: 9a2eb48ee628412ca12a5075ad9b8a44
msgid "`United Kingdom Silicone Gel Tape, Pads <https://gelmec.co.uk/family.php?page=58&ty=9&gr=9>`__ and `grommets <https://gelmec.co.uk/family.php?page=64&ty=19&gr=19>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:58
#: 1b20a400de3a41bab3697c43ac721d8f
msgid "`Moon Gel Pads <https://www.amazon.com/RTOM-Moongel-Damper-Pads-Original/dp/B00F2PKRJW/>`__ (also available in music stores). Caution: Moon Gel has been shown to fail in heat above 100 degrees Fahrenheit so it should be used cautiously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:63
#: c00f6352914647d29158b22730347d82
msgid "Secure the autopilot to the frame with 1cm) wide velcro retaining strap or a rubber band. Be careful the strap does not hold down the controller so securely that it interferes with the damping of the pads. Consider putting a layer of soft foam between the strap and the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:73
#: 1195939b5d354464b2add53c2fb9cfb4
msgid "FlameWheel F330 with PX4FMU Mounted on Intermediate platform"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:75
#: da5de75a3d544fd28e793f83dd11660c
msgid "The blog `Testing simple anti-vibration solutions for GoPro on an ArduCopter <https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/testing-simple-anti-vibration-solutions-for-gopro-on-an>`__ has a video demonstrating vibration isolation using Moon Gel on a Go-Pro camera."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:79
#: 065a4c5b0ab84602af34f23c63c7abfb
msgid "3D Printed Anti-Vibration Platform"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:81
#: 69f66618a6174a87bd43377bb28c10cd
msgid "GuyMcCaldin's 3D Printed vibration mount on `Thingiverse <https://www.thingiverse.com/thing:3794194>`__  using `dampers like these <https://www.amazon.com/MECCANIXITY-Anti-Vibration-Absorption-M3-5x13mm-Quadcopter/dp/B0BZNWDVF5?th=1>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:87
#: 45f91253cbbf4d168fa96c6df0d20812
msgid "The mount can be installed using double sided tape, or M3 screws."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:90
#: 012d7d96d7c2446f9c5569ab1d471b1e
msgid "3D Printed Anti-Vibration Platform for NAVIO2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:92
#: f9902128491f4584bafeda96a5a4f633
msgid "Anti-vibration for NAVIO2 can be easily 3D printed. It significantly simplifies mounting and eliminates vibrations. You can find STL files `here <https://docs.emlid.com/navio2/ardupilot/hardware-setup/#anti-vibration-mount>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:96
#: 4079928560ec417784d36791338db149
msgid "Anti-vibration with Navio2 mounted on frame:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:102
#: 961155a7d9db43aa8c1f8b65c3107bc6
msgid "O-ring Suspension Mount"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:104
#: b30d732e11dd4dfcb5c6a5542a9b72a1
msgid "Create a platform upon which to mount your autopilot with holes or screws on the four corners. Mount your autopilot on this board with double sided foam tape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:107
#: cc86c1ca5ce2429b9f4e10e219357ada
msgid "Mount 4 standoffs on the top of your frame spaced 1/10\" to 1/8\" further apart than the width of the platform upon which the control board has been mounted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:110
#: ca16ae11f46f42f88bc1620a041e31ed
msgid "Insert 1/16\" nylon O-rings through each corner of the autopilot and the standoffs so that the autopilot has no hard connections to the frame. Link `(Here!) <http://www.oringwarehouse.com/>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:114
#: 1f7c524784c24f38baffb855c65bd2cd
msgid "The overall O-ring diameter should be chosen to firmly retain the board while providing for light to moderate initial but rapidly snubbed movement of the board (generally 1/2\" to 3/4\" OD) and Silicone O-rings should generally damp better than Buna-N O-rings (Sizes 15 - 21) if you can acquire them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:125
#: 2f3c4526a07f45a2b13a926275a9f3f6
msgid "FlameWheel F450 O-Ring Suspension Platform Mount"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:127
#: f90ed2b5999c45eab5cfe5ccf58d0bd8
msgid "Vibrations are short coupled, so all that leaving excess corner clearance does is to require higher initial O-ring tension which reduces vibration damping responsiveness and allows the board to physically tilt more (which is undesirable as it throws the sensor to airframe relationship off)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:133
#: b59638225a604cdf9cbf1f49e1c9676e
msgid "The disadvantage to O-ring suspension versus Gel pads is that it is mechanically more complex and it requires tuning of both of O-ring diameter and cross section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:137
#: 706308bef0374004bddebd182724febb
msgid "You can combine O-ring and gel pad design by using an intermediate plate and benefit from dual rate damping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:141
#: 16770d0f7de24e05aa7ee220870f21fe
msgid "Ear Plug Mount"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:143
#: 851751ced2ab47a99d1b0fd8ff6a43b3
msgid "Purchase slow response silicon or urethane foam or PVC foam earplugs such as `these from 3M <https://www.3m.com/3M/en_US/company-us/all-3m-products/~/3M-E-A-R-Classic-Earplugs-390-1000-Uncorded-Value-Pack-2000-Pair-Case/?N=5002385+3294755285&rt=rud>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:145
#: 91f9cd875d8f4d22bd01b4e1cd28c828
msgid "Create a platform upon which to mount your autopilot with holes at the four corners. The holes should be large enough to allow the ear plugs to be inserted into them but not so loose that the board comes loose during hard landings. Ensure the holes are smooth so they do not cut into the ear plugs. Also keep the holes near the corners of your electronic module plate as possible to minimize unnecessary module movement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:152
#: 36358e54890b4ce9b9aa6576b529787e
msgid "Mount your autopilot on this board with double sided foam tape. Extra mass added to the board may improve vibration damping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:154
#: e350c0ae67a848c79b1d490a705d356c
msgid "Squeeze the earplugs through existing holes in the frame (or cut new holes) and the holes in the board upon which the autopilot is mounted. \"Tuning\" is possible by varying the amount of earplug left exposed in the middle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:163
#: e3ab1fbabc524cf0b89ee44d14d8db41
msgid "Ear Plug Vibration Mount"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:166
#: 20140a8960c443a89499314f3684dc37
msgid "Bulb Damper + Ear Plug Vibration Mount"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:168
#: a6bf7d3817644e209e14bb953adfd049
msgid "Mounting plate with a 100g soft rubber bulb type \"gimbal\" damper at each corner and a half a urethane foam earplug placed inside each one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:171
#: ef0ed9852fef49bb96f1c45b25361653
msgid "Gimbal bulb type dampers themselves can work in tension or compression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:173
#: e6b0af720d014fe6b668dab56ab296ac
msgid "The earplugs provide an additional damping medium with a different frequency damping range than the bulb dampers by themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:175
#: 39b026547aa74950b27917d523f76e1a
msgid "The ear plugs also stiffen the bulb mounts up a bit preventing excessive free motion being caused by normal flight maneuvers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:177
#: 245c59637f7741d1a7b3976390a5bd77
msgid "This was successful at damping a Flamewheel clone with flexible arms and over size 12\" propellers into the .05 G range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:184
#: ac65a44a1ab6481380adfaad5fc014b9
msgid "The autopilot is also mounted on anti-vibration grommets available from `McMaster Carr <https://www.mcmaster.com/vibration-damping-grommets>`__ (package of 25 each part #9311K64 recommended)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:185
#: 610f3f0096a443a490040460ed0363be
msgid "The 100G bulb type gimbal vibration dampers can be ordered direct from a variety of vendors: \\ `copter-rc.com <http://copter-rc.com/en/accessories/36-100g-av-ball-tension-damper.html>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:189
#: 4a088749c4354047bb64fca199e0d2f9
msgid "Advice for reducing vibrations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:191
#: d04887853e174bacb91ebee1823971cd
msgid "For copters the largest source of vibration is normally the blades passing over the arms but other sources of vibration also exist and may be reduced by following this advice:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:193
#: cfea8c73e55d4cab96775123d80434f7
msgid "Frame flex especially arm flex is a big cause of asynchronous vibration, Frame arms should be as rigid as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:196
#: fdc5a188425a416180d0eba8930cff43
msgid "Original DJI Flamewheel copters have sufficiently rigid injection molded arms, the many clones do not"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:197
#: 64e28873da8443efbb84f39681fcf349
msgid "Aluminum or carbon fibre arms twist and bend less which reduces vibrations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:198
#: 4e869855faa14f85ac866d0cf2aaa2e8
msgid "Copters with injection molded exoskeletons or arms like the Iris are sufficiently rigid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:199
#: 34424a60b2cc49c985fa01528882ea55
msgid "Cheap, light frames tend to flex more than high quality stronger ones and the heavier you load the copter the more flex it gets (not good)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:201
#: a21a6a887bf347429524b868370f185a
msgid "Motor to frame arm and frame arm to central hub mounts need to be secure and flex free (sometimes a problem for carbon tube arms)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:202
#: 57ee7babb6244a1ebfa4034c4fdccaa5
msgid "Motors need to run smoothly (bearings not worn-out or \"screeching\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:203
#: c5ab215016064eb5b595a5e81ad0f68b
msgid "Prop adapters connecting the propellers to the motors need to be concentric and very straight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:204
#: d79d1b4c8d8f4f1eb0d397edb40df586
msgid "Propellers should be fully balanced using a good manual prop balancer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:205
#: b4255ee33b7d4d08bdfd6c6fbd5921d0
msgid "Motor balancing (or really well factory balanced motors like T-Motor) can have a major effect"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:206
#: 9145d2a642d940d08ee817828d60cba6
msgid "Propellers that are not well matched to the frame and weight or do  not have the same flex for CCW and CW are very problematic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:207
#: 3094bc7ea8da40548f57e8c1b66ad364
msgid "Good propellers vibrate less"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:208
#: adefa1cadf9448d19e5e358c212514bf
msgid "Carbon fiber props are rigid and vibrate less which reduces vibration but are very sharp which is a major safety hazard"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:211
#: 2069841c467a435cbe87be86933c54fd
msgid "Summary of the vibrations that should be damped"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:213
#: 636924efda1e4679a72562ba86ff482b
msgid "The vibration frequency and amplitude we primarily need to reduce is a characteristic of the motor / prop units turning at flight speed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:215
#: 15f167dd5f2543b583d19a4645c0f20d
msgid "That is, it is a fairly high frequency with fairly low amplitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:216
#: 3fbf2ec3a13b4f888c841a119cc965f9
msgid "This requires that we provide a short coupled damping and isolation range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:217
#: b00135c3d0cb48a08aa843975a32e7a7
msgid "The board itself does not need to have nor benefit from a range of motion that exceeds the amplitude of the vibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:219
#: 5f419e0d485c4e18834de0ad594f254e
msgid "Because the board does not apply any force to the airframe, the only thing we need to be concerned with Damping / Isolating is the weight (mass) of the board itself plus the forces applied to it by airframe's normal flight maneuvering."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:223
#: eed1e6f98b8d4a45885f572dc7de8618
msgid "Since excellent broad frequency range, high damping materials are available our biggest concern will be to use the proper amount of them to optimally damp our autopilot (too much is just as bad as too little)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:227
#: e0dd80e3d7a44bc1ba0422da0282822a
msgid "Combining the autopilot and receiver onto a separate vibration damped electronics module \"plate\" or enclosure can increase the mass of the module making it easier to damp effectively as well as reducing the interconnecting wiring and making the whole system more modular."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:234
#: 7482bcfeced04fb1ace72fa41b653d2e
msgid "Additional Vibration Reduction Considerations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:236
#: 959ed19eb62743a88e8a9a83ca5a569e
msgid "`Hard Disk Drive Anti-Vibration Grommets <http://www.frozencpu.com/products/8869/scr-138/Hard_Disk_Drive_Anti-Vibration_Screw_Shoulder_Screw_Type.html?tl=g33c113&id=gdASxUFI>`__. can provide sufficient or supplemental vibration reduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:238
#: 6e14afdc3a67401e919398d94f02028a
msgid "Significant gains in vibration isolation can also be realized by using a high flex wire and strain relief approach to all wires connected to the autopilot (and using the minimum number of wires necessary as well)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:242
#: bfa42dbc3919450cbcbdb03b7bec3b19
msgid "Some frames have lower than normal vibration characteristics due to frame stiffness / flex and isolated centralized mass can greatly influence motor/prop vibration transfer to the central fight controller."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:246
#: c9aecf4fc1fa49babf23eb3a97df954c
msgid "Isolation and damping can be improved somewhat by sandwiching the autopilot / enclosure between damping pads on both sides in about twenty percent compression. 30 durometer Sorbothane is actually specified at 15 to 20 percent compression for optimal damping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:251
#: 6dc5b63fcf4b4410b19c34ad88a7ed9b
msgid "Although 30 durometer Sorbothane seems an excellent candidate, experience indicates that it becomes permanently compressed and is not as effective at vibration reduction as the Gel solutions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:254
#: 17dc8e29ce1f4147b1d9a7196e93e7d3
msgid "A link to a Blog about the first APM anti-vibration mounting system to achieve 0.05 G damping (2/20/2013 improved to 0.02 G), a dual zone isolation system, combining O-ring suspension and silicone pad is `(Here!) <https://diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/very-good-anti-vibration-results-with-following-apm2-5-mount-quad>`__"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:258
#: e9d3c8c142904238970b8bd72772817e
msgid "Motor balancing can also reduce vibration and especially so for cheaper or larger motors. Balancing involves:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:261
#: a87622cb702643448652eaad783cec56
msgid "Tightly fasten a small tie wrap around a motor (WITH NO PROP), trim off the extended tab and spin it up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:263
#: 784879d1e5614f82831720d8449a3f34
msgid "Try multiple times, each time turning the tie wrap on the motor housing a bit until the vibration reduces or goes away."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:265
#: 55d89da9507c4f31b55485c195a3ed57
msgid "A small piece of Scotch tape can be re-positioned instead of the tie wrap if desired or for smaller motors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:267
#: 1aba48d1483c40baa97c8508164adc48
msgid "When you locate the spot where there is the least vibration (and you should be able to hear it), mark the spot directly under the clasp of the tie-wrap with a felt pen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:270
#: d6735cca387e4953bdf7e078e0f41db0
msgid "Add a small dot of hot glue gun glue where the Tie-Wrap clasp was and increase the glue a bit at a time till the vibration is minimized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:273
#: 484b6ab7e06746f5a18c24aba0c02d38
msgid "If you put too much glue on it can be removed with an X-acto knife."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:275
#: 4f9f4254c4ea4d7bbcbfa67ba654f7c4
msgid "Camera Mounts also need to be effectively isolated and damped from vibration, but they already have a number of \"soft\" mounting solutions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:278
#: ac6e459d9ab142edae09b2623fb39c5d
msgid "The camera servos need to be vibration isolated as well, either in the isolated camera mount itself or with their own vibration reduction solution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:281
#: df67626cedcf4258a1b69504cebf42fa
msgid "You should use high quality ball joints on your camera servo arms and adequate bearings or bushings in the mount itself with zero free play to prevent inertial slop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:284
#: 4037976bfb78403f874baa1c844619f9
msgid "Quality servos without free play are also a must for precision camera work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:286
#: 7ac3f148241c4ae6a478649ea898c224
msgid "At this point in time it seems that the more rigid the frame the better because frame flex introduces undesirable mechanical delay (hysteresis) in translating motor induced actions to the centrally located autopilot. (Do NOT shock mount the motor Arms)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:290
#: 11cdbe5ee5a34ad28267d57834c40214
msgid "The amount and type of damping medium needs to be carefully matched to the weight (mass) of the item we are trying to isolate as well as the frequency and amplitude of the vibrations we are seeking to damp. We are trying to isolate an autopilot that weighs less than 2 ounces and this is a very small mass."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:295
#: c95e8509654a4fd484ba375262dee0e5
msgid "Virtually all off the shelf solutions (either pad or stud type) are designed for an isolated mass that would weigh at least 5 to 10 times what an average autopilot weighs for optimal effectiveness. This includes all pre-made Sorbothane, Alpha gel, EAR, memory foam or other silicone or urethane gel or foam mounts as well as Lord Micro mounts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:301
#: 164eabd6492a4d5ca7cfa1357650014a
msgid "A threaded stud or sleeve type mount gel mount properly designed for the mass of our autopilot or electronics module undergoing the stress's of normal flight would be a much better long term solution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:307
#: 019f7dc1edec4c2f980b01eb14c3df5b
msgid "Terminology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:309
#: ec740905ad404d16bf1d6906d81b7e5a
msgid "The methods used will typically incorporate both damping and isolation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:311
#: b8bfbdd5755c491fa1965d0a89c2f253
msgid "*Isolation* is simple undamped (spring or rubber band support) which allows the movement of the isolated object largely separate from the containing object (Automobile spring for instance)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-vibration-damping.rst:314
#: c69b4d40f7f94b57bdeee2006708f5b6
msgid "*Damping* is the conversion of vibration into heat energy by a shock absorbing medium (automobile shock absorber for instance)."
msgstr ""

